---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { scenes } from '../../../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { signs } from '../../../data/signs';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'The 12 Signs', href: '/explore/signs/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The 12 Zodiac Signs. Styles of expression"
  description="The twelve zodiac signs describe styles of expression, not twelve kinds of people. Learn elements, modalities, rulers, and how signs modify planets and houses in context."
  path="/explore/signs/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
      <h1>The 12 signs</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Signs describe style: the manner in which a planet or house expresses itself. They are not
        twelve personality types. Every birth chart contains all twelve signs, and each one takes on
        meaning through the planets, houses, rulers, and relationships connected to it.
      </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.signs.src}
        srcset={scenes.signs.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.signs.alt}
        width={scenes.signs.width}
        height={scenes.signs.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-8);">
      <h2>A sign tells you how, not the whole story</h2>
      <p>
        Aries can describe a direct, initiating style; Taurus a stabilizing one; Gemini a curious,
        connective one. But the sign alone does not tell you what is being expressed, where in life
        it is operating, or what other parts of the chart are modifying it. Venus in Aries and
        Saturn in Aries share a style without doing the same job.
      </p>
      <p>
        The twelve signs also form a beautifully ordered system: four elements—fire, earth, air,
        and water—crossed with three modalities—cardinal, fixed, and mutable. Learning that structure
        is more useful than memorizing twelve disconnected lists of traits.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="container grid grid--3">
      {
        signs.map((s) => (
          <article class="card">
            <span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">{s.glyph}</span>
            <h2 style="font-size: var(--text-xl); margin: 0;">
              <a href={`/explore/signs/${s.slug}/`}>{s.name}</a>
            </h2>
            <p class="card__meta">
              {s.element} · {s.modality} · ruled by {s.ruler}
            </p>
            <p style="margin: 0;">{s.keywords.join(' · ')}</p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Learn the pattern underneath the signs</h2>
      <p>
        Start with <a href="/explore/elements-and-modalities/">elements and modalities</a> to see
        the architecture beneath the zodiac. Then notice which <a href="/explore/planets/">planet</a>
        is expressing through a sign, which <a href="/explore/houses/">area of life</a> is involved,
        and what <a href="/explore/aspects/">relationships</a> modify it. A sign becomes much more
        meaningful when you stop asking "What is this sign like?" and start asking "What is this
        sign doing here?"
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
